  "text": "6.3 Okay, Alika. Should we look at your project now? Yes, okay. You've done a lot of reading for this, which is great. I can see you wrote a lot of information down. Yes, but I haven't finished yet. I ran out of time, but I read as much as I could about 103 different reports. I took part in the discussions on this with students from my study group. I studied the way people work. Good. Can you tell me what you learned? Well, I looked at how people spend time at work. I was interested in how people wasted time and why they didn't work hard at their jobs. I mean the reasons why they didn't focus on their work. I found out a lot of interesting information. For example, almost half of the workers wasted time on the internet. They were surfing the internet or writing personal emails. It was 48 percent of workers, in fact. 31.5 percent also said they spent time talking to colleagues instead of working. And a little under half that amount, 15 percent, said they took longer coffee and lunch breaks than they should. Only 5 percent said they spent time texting friends and making plans for after work. Good. And did you find out the reasons why? Yes. Approximately half said they didn't work hard because they weren't happy in their jobs. I see. And about a third, 33.5 percent, thought they didn't earn a good enough salary. They said they didn't have enough money for holidays or nice clothes. They had just enough money to get by, so they didn't work as hard as they could. Some people, about 19 percent, said they had to work too many hours. So what are some effects of people not working very hard? Hmm. If people aren't working hard, companies don't get what they are paying for. Can you give me some examples? Well, when people don't work hard, productivity goes down. Because productivity depends on how fast workers make things or provide services, if workers are not working hard, the company doesn't make a profit. Then they can't pay higher salaries.",
